<div class="section" id="s1">
  <span class="section-label">Section 1</span>
  <h2 class="section-heading">Introduction to Design Thinking</h2>

  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Design thinking for problem solving</h3>
    <p>Five stages from the Stanford d.school: empathise, define, ideate, prototype, test. Humans are at the centre. Problem statements have four parts: the problem, the persona, the outcome, and how to measure it. No solutions proposed in the problem statement — the process leads to conclusions without assumptions.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Iterative — repeat as many times as needed</li>
      <li>Applies to any problem involving humans, not just software</li>
      <li>Problem statement format: problem + persona + outcome + measurement</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="placeholder-label">Screenshot placeholder — design thinking diagram</p>
    <div class="placeholder md"><p>Add screenshot of the five-stage design thinking diagram here<br><br>Filename suggestion: design-thinking-five-stages.png</p></div>
    <div class="how-to-add">To add: <code>mv ~/Desktop/design-thinking-five-stages.png ~/Documents/Code/portfolio/src/assets/images/</code> then import and use <code>&lt;Image class="full-image" src={...}&gt;</code></div>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Case study: SaaS product</h3>
    <p>Course uses an HR consultancy building a SaaS tool as the running example. Starts with an ambiguous brief — no clear direction. Design thinking is the tool for navigating from vague brief to product direction.</p>
    <p>Key point: ambiguous problems are normal in product development. Design thinking connects user behaviour to product experience.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section" id="s2">
  <span class="section-label">Section 2</span>
  <h2 class="section-heading">Product Definition, Ideation, and Frameworks</h2>

  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Design thinking for product development</h3>
    <p>Design fits in the first three phases of product delivery: discovery, define, design. Design works ahead of development — sometimes by a few sprints. Poor planning causes usability issues, poor metrics, and limited scalability.</p>
    <p>In agile: design requirements established, design created, tested with users, then handed off as epics broken into stories.</p>
    <p class="placeholder-label">Screenshot placeholder — design in agile diagram</p>
    <div class="placeholder sm"><p>Add screenshot of design process in agile delivery here</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Understand customer behaviors and goals</h3>
    <p>Qualitative + quantitative data together give the full picture. Analytics shows what is happening. Interviews reveal why. Neither is enough alone.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Survey first to segment roles</li>
      <li>Interviews with representative sample from each role</li>
      <li>Contextual inquiry to observe what people forget to articulate</li>
      <li>Spend 20 minutes after each interview capturing key takeaways</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Identify and synthesize customer insights</h3>
    <p>Affinity mapping: one insight per sticky note, cluster into themes, let themes emerge from the data rather than imposing them upfront. Physical or digital both work — Miro, FigJam, whiteboard.</p>
    <p>If themes are not clear, go back and interview more people. Synthesis reveals research gaps as much as patterns.</p>
    <p class="placeholder-label">Screenshot placeholder — affinity mapping example</p>
    <div class="placeholder sm"><p>Add screenshot of affinity mapping from the course here</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Develop customer personas</h3>
    <p>Personas are research-based illustrative models. Standard components: name, occupation, goals, tasks, pain points, opportunities. Provisional personas work without research — best guesses supplemented by data.</p>
    <p>Primary use: alignment. Gives the whole team a shared reference when making design and product decisions.</p>
    <p class="placeholder-label">Screenshot placeholder — persona example</p>
    <div class="placeholder sm"><p>Add screenshot of persona template from the course here</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Visualize customer journey maps</h3>
    <p>Journey maps capture: phases, activities, pain points, emotions, quotes, opportunities, metrics. Primary use: identifying gaps and pain points. Research-based, can be built as sticky notes or in Miro.</p>
    <p>If information is missing, go back and talk to users. Journey maps can start as a team exercise and be made more visual over time.</p>
    <p class="placeholder-label">Screenshot placeholder — journey map example</p>
    <div class="placeholder md"><p>Add screenshot of journey map from the course here</p></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section" id="s3">
  <span class="section-label">Section 3</span>
  <h2 class="section-heading">Uncovering and Identifying Business Problems</h2>

  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>User centered product requirements</h3>
    <p>Requirements derived from user research rather than business assumptions. User-centred approach uses iterative feedback from real users. Traditional project management is business-first and linear. User-centred requirements ensure people will actually want to use the product.</p>
    <p>The research-to-requirement connection is direct: the recruiter's pain point about feedback coordination directly generates the requirement for feedback templates and reminders.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Ideation for user experience</h3>
    <p>Divergent process first — generate as many ideas as possible without constraints, time-boxed to 15-20 minutes. Low fidelity sketches to move fast. Group ideation with dot voting. Then converge — pick strongest ideas and add detail.</p>
    <p>Key principle: do not constrain yourself too early. Solutions emerge from challenging assumptions.</p>
    <p class="placeholder-label">Screenshot placeholder — ideation sketches</p>
    <div class="scroll-placeholder">
      <div class="item"><p>Sketch 1<br>Add ideation sketches from course</p></div>
      <div class="item"><p>Sketch 2<br>Dashboard layout ideation</p></div>
      <div class="item"><p>Sketch 3<br>Alternative layout concept</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Plan user flows</h3>
    <p>User flows map every decision point and connection before screens are designed. Boxes and arrows. Shows how pages connect, what decisions users face, how personas interact with the same system.</p>
    <p>Distinction from site maps: flows show decisions and logic. Site maps show pages and views without decisions. Both are needed.</p>
    <p class="placeholder-label">Screenshot placeholder — user flow diagram</p>
    <div class="placeholder sm"><p>Add screenshot of user flow from the course here</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Gathering feedback</h3>
    <p>Stay low fidelity as long as possible so feedback shapes the design before you invest in it. Sketches are enough to get meaningful reactions. Ask open-ended questions, avoid leading questions, let participants sketch their own ideas if the design is wrong.</p>
    <p>Key: you just need enough to get reactions. Fail fast is the mechanism — get wrong answers early when they are cheap to fix.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section" id="s4">
  <span class="section-label">Section 4</span>
  <h2 class="section-heading">Usability and Design Paradigms</h2>

  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Create an information architecture</h3>
    <p>Information architecture: how people find and move through a product — global navigation, page-level navigation, search, site maps. A good IA is invisible. When it works, users just move through the product without thinking about the structure.</p>
    <p>Global navigation is accessible everywhere. Page-level navigation organises content within a defined area. Site map tracks all pages and views — different from a user flow.</p>
    <p class="placeholder-label">Screenshot placeholder — IA diagram</p>
    <div class="placeholder sm"><p>Add screenshot of site map / IA diagram from the course here</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Cognitive load and Gestalt principles</h3>
    <p>Cognitive load: the mental effort required to process a page. Too many steps, too much content, poor layout all increase cognitive load. Gestalt principles are how the brain naturally organises visual information — design with them, not against them.</p>

    <div class="principle">
      <span class="name">Proximity</span>
      <span class="desc">Elements close together are perceived as related. White space is the active use of this — creates grouping without visible borders.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="principle">
      <span class="name">Similarity</span>
      <span class="desc">Elements that look alike are perceived as functioning alike. Consistent colour, shape, size signal relationships.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="principle">
      <span class="name">Closure</span>
      <span class="desc">The brain completes incomplete shapes. A line graph does not need to be fully solid to read as a continuous line.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="principle">
      <span class="name">Enclosure</span>
      <span class="desc">A bordered or bounded area is perceived as a group. Cards and containers work because of this.</span>
    </div>

    <p class="placeholder-label" style="margin-top:1rem">Screenshot placeholder — Gestalt principles diagram</p>
    <div class="placeholder md"><p>Add screenshot of Gestalt principles diagrams from the course here<br><br>The course shows each principle visually — proximity, similarity, closure, enclosure</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Usability factors in the user experience</h3>
    <p>Usability: ease and effectiveness of achieving goals. Four factors:</p>
    <div class="principle">
      <span class="name">Design patterns</span>
      <span class="desc">Repeatable solutions to common problems. Reduce cognitive load because users already know them. Must suit the specific context.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="principle">
      <span class="name">Information architecture</span>
      <span class="desc">Content labelling, page structure, organisation. Poor IA makes things unfindable regardless of visual design quality.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="principle">
      <span class="name">Flow</span>
      <span class="desc">Logical sequence of steps. If order does not match user expectations, usability suffers even with well-designed individual screens.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="principle">
      <span class="name">Visual design</span>
      <span class="desc">Colour, typography, iconography, layout. Affects both desirability and usability. How something looks influences whether it feels usable.</span>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:.75rem;font-size:.88rem;color:#666">Measurement methods: heuristic evaluations, surveys, formal usability testing.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Fitts's law, affordance, and guiding principles</h3>
    <p><strong>Fitts's Law:</strong> time to reach a target depends on its size and distance. Interactive elements must be large enough to tap or click accurately — especially on mobile.</p>

    <div class="principle">
      <span class="name">Affordance</span>
      <span class="desc">Shape or appearance suggests how something should be used. Shadows and gradients on buttons signal they are pressable.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="principle">
      <span class="name">Consistency</span>
      <span class="desc">Same patterns, fonts, colours, iconography throughout. Users learn the system once and apply that knowledge everywhere. Core argument for design systems.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="principle">
      <span class="name">Mental models</span>
      <span class="desc">Users bring existing expectations from other products. The shopping cart is the classic example. Match existing mental models for immediate intuitiveness.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="principle">
      <span class="name">Feedback</span>
      <span class="desc">System responds to every action — loading indicators, hover states, haptic responses. Users need to know their action was received.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="principle">
      <span class="name">Error prevention</span>
      <span class="desc">Design so errors are unlikely. When they occur, users can reverse them without damage.</span>
    </div>

    <p class="placeholder-label" style="margin-top:1rem">Screenshot placeholder — Fitts's law diagram</p>
    <div class="placeholder sm"><p>Add screenshot of Fitts's law and affordance diagrams from the course here</p></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section" id="s5">
  <span class="section-label">Section 5</span>
  <h2 class="section-heading">User Interface Design and Prototyping</h2>

  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Fundamentals of user interface design</h3>
    <p>Visual hierarchy established through position, size, weight, and colour. Important things go first, top to bottom. Without hierarchy, users cannot tell what to look at first.</p>
    <div class="principle">
      <span class="name">Typography</span>
      <span class="desc">Size and weight create reading order. Same weight across a page makes everything equally important — nothing stands out.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="principle">
      <span class="name">Colour</span>
      <span class="desc">Use to draw focus or de-emphasise. Never colour alone for meaning — support with text and icons. Too much colour increases cognitive load.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="principle">
      <span class="name">Layout and grid</span>
      <span class="desc">12-column grid is the standard for web. White space at grid level separates columns. White space within modules separates elements inside them.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="principle">
      <span class="name">White space</span>
      <span class="desc">Not empty space — active design element. Reinforces proximity and grouping. Makes relationships between elements legible.</span>
    </div>

    <p class="placeholder-label" style="margin-top:1rem">Screenshot placeholder — visual hierarchy examples</p>
    <div class="scroll-placeholder">
      <div class="item"><p>Position hierarchy example</p></div>
      <div class="item"><p>Size hierarchy example</p></div>
      <div class="item"><p>Typography hierarchy example</p></div>
      <div class="item"><p>Colour hierarchy example</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>User interface components and elements</h3>
    <p>UI components — buttons, forms, radio buttons, sliders, drawers — are the building blocks of interfaces. Design systems collect these with documentation covering what they are, when to use them, when not to, error states, anatomy, and context guidance.</p>
    <p>Components allow code reuse and visual consistency simultaneously. A Figma component maps to a coded component in engineering — shared source of truth.</p>
    <p class="placeholder-label">Screenshot placeholder — design system component example</p>
    <div class="placeholder sm"><p>Add screenshot of Microsoft Fluent 2 component documentation from the course here</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Low fidelity design with Figma</h3>
    <p>Paper Wireframe Kit from Figma Community — intentionally low fidelity so people are not distracted by visual elements. Work loosely, not pixel-perfect. Goal at this stage: get the flow and navigation structure right before layout decisions are made.</p>
    <p>Navigation structure first, content second. Start with a community wireframe kit so you are not building from scratch at the low fidelity stage.</p>
    <p class="placeholder-label">Screenshot placeholder — low fidelity wireframe</p>
    <div class="placeholder md"><p>Add screenshot of the paper wireframe kit in Figma from the course here</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Interactive prototyping with Figma</h3>
    <p>Connect screens with triggers and animations. Smart Animate: detects matching layers between frames, animates the differences — reinforces the feedback principle. The user sees that their action caused a reaction.</p>
    <p>Four screens with a few connected interactions is enough to test with users. A prototype does not need to be fully built to be testable.</p>
    <p class="placeholder-label">Screenshot placeholder — Figma prototype connections</p>
    <div class="placeholder sm"><p>Add screenshot of Figma prototype mode showing screen connections here</p></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section" id="principles">
  <span class="section-label">Quick reference</span>
  <h2 class="section-heading">Psychology principles summary</h2>

  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Hick's Law</span><span class="desc">More choices = longer to decide. Reduce options at key decision points.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Fitts's Law</span><span class="desc">Target acquisition time depends on size and distance. Make interactive elements large enough to hit.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Miller's Law</span><span class="desc">Working memory holds 7 ± 2 items. Chunk information into groups of 5–9.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Jakob's Law</span><span class="desc">Users spend most time on other sites. Design to meet existing expectations.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Cognitive Load</span><span class="desc">Mental effort has a limit. Good design reduces extraneous load so users focus on the actual task.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Proximity</span><span class="desc">Elements close together are perceived as related. White space creates grouping without borders.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Similarity</span><span class="desc">Elements that look alike are perceived as functioning alike.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Closure</span><span class="desc">The brain completes incomplete shapes.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Enclosure</span><span class="desc">Bounded areas are perceived as groups. Cards work because of this.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Von Restorff Effect</span><span class="desc">The different element is most remembered. Primary CTA buttons use a different colour for this reason.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Zeigarnik Effect</span><span class="desc">Incomplete tasks are remembered better. Progress bars create tension that motivates completion.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Peak-End Rule</span><span class="desc">Experiences are judged by their peak and their end. The confirmation screen matters as much as the form.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Affordance</span><span class="desc">Appearance suggests function. Shadows and gradients on buttons signal they are pressable.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Aesthetic-Usability</span><span class="desc">Attractive interfaces are perceived as easier to use, even when they are not.</span></div>
</div>

<div class="section" id="ui-fundamentals">
  <span class="section-label">Quick reference</span>
  <h2 class="section-heading">UI fundamentals summary</h2>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Visual hierarchy</span><span class="desc">Position, size, weight, colour. Important things go first. Without hierarchy, nothing stands out.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Typography</span><span class="desc">Size and weight create reading order. Bold and size differences create the sequence.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Colour</span><span class="desc">Draw focus or de-emphasise. Never colour alone. Support with text and icons for accessibility.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Grid</span><span class="desc">12-column grid standard for web. Align elements to the grid. White space separates columns.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">White space</span><span class="desc">Active design element, not empty space. Reinforces grouping, reduces cognitive load.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Design patterns</span><span class="desc">Repeatable solutions to common problems. Reduce cognitive load through familiarity.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Consistency</span><span class="desc">Same patterns throughout. Users learn once and apply everywhere. Core argument for design systems.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Feedback</span><span class="desc">Every action gets a response — loading states, hover states, confirmations.</span></div>
</div>

</div>
</div>

<script>
const links = document.querySelectorAll('.sidebar nav a');
const sections = document.querySelectorAll('.section[id]');
const observer = new IntersectionObserver(entries => {
  entries.forEach(e => {
    if (e.isIntersecting) {
      links.forEach(l => l.classList.remove('active'));
      const link = document.querySelector(`.sidebar nav a[href="#${e.target.id}"]`);
      if (link) link.classList.add('active');
    }
  });
}, { rootMargin: '-10% 0px -75% 0px' });
sections.forEach(s => observer.observe(s));
links.forEach(link => {
  link.addEventListener('click', e => {
    e.preventDefault();
    const id = link.getAttribute('href').slice(1);
    document.getElementById(id)?.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' });
  });
});
</script>
